meta {
  name: 01 Activate
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/student-activations
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "key": "{{activationKey}}",
    "password": "a-student-password",
    "deviceId": "student-device-1",
    "deviceName": "Android"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
}

docs {
  Public, and deliberately unauthenticated — a student arrives with a key and no
  account they can yet sign into. The key IS the credential, which is why this is
  the one public endpoint carrying a tight per-address rate limit.

  Run `Center Students / 02 Provision student` first; it captures
  `activationKey` for you.

  Returns an ordinary student token pair. From here the student is a normal
  Lerniqo user who happens to belong to a center.

  The checks that matter:

  - RUN IT TWICE. The second must fail with 400 ACTIVATION_KEY_INVALID. The key
    is consumed by a predicated update, so a replay matches zero rows.

  - `"studentId": "someone-else"` or `"centerId": "a-center"` -> 400. The key
    alone decides which account is activated; a caller cannot name one.

  - a password under 8 characters -> 400. Activation must not be a weaker door
    into an account than registration is.

  THE TRIAL CLOCK starts here, on the FIRST activation in a center. Check
  `Center Subscription / 01 Get subscription` before and after:

      before   status TRIAL_PENDING, trialStartedAt null
      after    status TRIAL,         trialEndsAt = +30 days

  Then provision and activate a SECOND student and check again. The timestamps
  must be unchanged — a second student does not buy the center another thirty
  days.

  The password set here belongs to the student. Their center never learns it,
  and cannot re-key the account now that it is active.
}
